The hot path to Claude is one HTTPS POST and a stream of six event types. Every SDK wraps that in thousands of lines, and the wrapping costs you: in cold start, in memory, and in the milliseconds between the model finishing a thought and your code acting on it. For a chat window none of that matters. For a CLI that should feel instant, a Lambda that is billed from the first millisecond, or an agent loop making ten thousand calls a day, it is the only part of the latency you control.
This series writes the path by hand, one language a week, one file each, and measures what each one adds. This post is the floor: a complete client in bash and curl, in one file, with no jq, that streams the reply as it arrives, completes a tool call round trip, and reports API errors properly. Everything that follows is measured against it, and against the official SDKs.
The code and the harness are at github.com/AndyCross/claude-hot-path, and the leaderboard lives at /claude-hot-path/.
The spec
Every client in the series has to pass the same seven points, and a harness checks them rather than me reading the code.
Read the key from ANTHROPIC_API_KEY and the base URL from ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL. Nothing else from the environment.
Read the prompt from stdin.
POST /v1/messages with the x-api-key, anthropic-version: 2023-06-01 and content-type: application/json headers, and a body with model, max_tokens, stream: true, messages and tools.
Print each text_delta as it arrives. The harness checks that the first visible byte reached stdout before the mock finished sending the stream, so buffering the reply, or flushing only on newlines, fails.
Declare one tool, local_time, which takes an IANA timezone. When the stream ends with stop_reason: "tool_use", assemble the input_json_delta fragments, run the tool, send the tool_result back in a second request with the assistant turn echoed, and stream that reply too.
On a non-2xx response, print the body's error.message to stderr and exit non-zero.
One source file. HTTP and TLS from the platform where it has them. Where it does not (C, Zig), one named library, which counts towards size.
Point 5 is the one that separates a client from a curl wrapper. Tool use is where the protocol stops being request and response and becomes a conversation you have to maintain, and the tool's arguments arrive as fragments of JSON that you assemble yourself.
What comes down the wire
With stream: true the response is text/event-stream. Each event is an event: line, a data: line carrying JSON, and a blank line. Here is the shape of the first turn, from a real recording, trimmed:
event: message_start
data: {"type":"message_start","message":{"id":"msg_...","model":"claude-opus-5-5",...}}

event: content_block_start
data: {"type":"content_block_start","index":0,"content_block":{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_...","name":"local_time","input":{}}}

event: ping
data: {"type": "ping"}

event: content_block_delta
data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":0,"delta":{"type":"input_json_delta","partial_json":"{\"timezone\""}}

event: content_block_delta
data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":0,"delta":{"type":"input_json_delta","partial_json":": \"Europe/London\"}"}}

event: content_block_stop
data: {"type":"content_block_stop","index":0}

event: message_delta
data: {"type":"message_delta","delta":{"stop_reason":"tool_use"},"usage":{"output_tokens":56}}

event: message_stop
data: {"type":"message_stop"}
Two things I had wrong before I recorded it. The model went straight to the tool call with no preamble text, so a client that assumes a text block always comes first is wrong. And there is a ping event in the middle that the documentation mentions and nobody's mental model includes. A client has to ignore event types it does not know.
The six you act on are message_start, content_block_start, content_block_delta, content_block_stop, message_delta and message_stop. Deltas come in two kinds, text_delta with a text field and input_json_delta with a partial_json field, and the index says which content block they belong to.
The client
The whole thing is 93 lines. The interesting parts follow, and the rest is in the repo.
JSON without jq means encoding strings yourself. The prompt comes from stdin and could contain anything, so backslash, quote and the control characters have to be escaped:
json_str() {
  local s=$1
  s=${s//\\/\\\\}; s=${s//\"/\\\"}
  s=${s//$'\n'/\\n}; s=${s//$'\r'/\\r}; s=${s//$'\t'/\\t}
  printf '"%s"' "$s"
}
Decoding goes the other way. The text deltas arrive as JSON strings, escapes and all, and bash's printf '%b' happens to understand \n, \t, \\ and \uXXXX, so after swapping \" for " you can hand it the string:
json_unstr() {
  local s=$1
  s=${s//\\\"/\"}; s=${s//\\\//\/}
  printf '%b' "$s"
}
