I have been building a series of pages where the computer generates the pattern: tin-glazed tiles painted by rule, cut tile and poured cement, heraldry, knotwork, a piece that breaks another piece, brick in raking light, the seventeen ways a wall can repeat and iron and silk. We've been travelling round Europe, mostly round Spain, borrowing from the art and the history of each place. This one is from England, which has its own tile, made by pressing a pattern into one clay and filling it with another.
Rosa y Granada
Rosa y Granada came first, in October, and never had a post of its own. Catherine of Aragon arrived in England in 1501 with the pomegranate of Granada as her badge, and for twenty-odd years it was carved and painted beside the Tudor rose wherever the king's works went. The page puts the two on a floor in the English medieval way, which is the inlaid tile. A red earthenware tile was pressed with a wooden stamp, the recess filled with white pipeclay slip, the surplus scraped off level, and the whole glazed with lead and fired. The lead glaze turns the white yellow and the red a deep brown. There are two colours and no brush, so the only hand in it is the stamp, cut once and pressed a thousand times, and the slip, which goes on thick in one place and thin in another.
On the page, an inlaid tile is two operations. A recess is filled with another clay, and a line of the body is left standing between two fills. Every painter on the page is one or the other. The rose is five heart-shaped petals inlaid in slip with a smaller rose of five more left standing in the red body inside it, which is how you get a red rose on a white one with only one colour to fill with. The pomegranate is the one traced for Leonor: its silhouette goes in as slip and the drawing's black lines are reserved in red.
Six hundred years later
Inlaid tiles died out in the sixteenth century and came back in the nineteenth. Samuel Wright of Shelton patented a way of making them in 1830, Herbert Minton bought a share of the patent in 1835, and A. W. N. Pugin wanted floors for the Gothic churches he was building and, from the late 1840s, for the new Palace of Westminster. Minton made them to his designs. They were called encaustic, from the Greek for burnt in, and within a generation they were in every church porch, town hall and hallway in the country.
The idea is the same and nearly everything around it changed. The body was pressed in a plaster mould and the hollows filled with liquid clay. There were four or five colours instead of two: red, buff, black, blue, sometimes white or green. The surface was left unglazed. The inlay went deep enough to outlast a century of boots, where the slip on a lot of medieval tiles was thin enough to walk off. And the tiles came out of the factory by the hundred thousand, all one size, to be set out with a string line by a contractor rather than bedded in lime by a mason.
One file, two recipes
The medieval material lived in Rosa y Granada's page, because nothing else needed it. Encaustic does, and a material that two pages share belongs in a file of its own rather than in the engine, which every tile page imports. The engine takes a material object with hooks for how a shape is filled, how the ground is laid, how far a tile strays from its cell, what the joint is and what age does, and it doesn't need to know that two of its pages are made of clay.
So the stamp operations, the rose and the lily moved into clay-assets/clay.js, and the material became two recipes. Each one says what the clay looks like up close (the colour functions are trimmed here):
const RECIPES = {
  // white slip in red clay under lead, 1250 to 1550
  medieval: {
    mottle: mottleOf(1501, 60, [4, 14], dark, light),
    speck: speckOf(1254, 160, [0.3, 0.8], dark, light),
    // the glaze pools against the edge of the inlay as a dark hairline
    pool: (pal, col) => hexA(pal.tint, 0.45),
    poolW: 1.1,
  },
  // clays pressed into clay in a mould, 1840 to 1900
  victorian: {
    mottle: mottleOf(1840, 40, [6, 18], dark, light),
    speck: speckOf(1851, 110, [0.25, 0.5], dark, light),
    // two plastic clays pressed together bleed a hair into each other
    pool: (pal, col) => hexA(col, 0.5),
    poolW: 0.7,
  },
};
The medieval one is heavier everywhere: more mottle where the slip went on thick and thin, more iron coming up through the glaze, a dark line where the lead pooled against the inlay. The Victorian one is flatter and finer, and the line at the edge of an inlay is the inlay's own colour, a hair of one clay pressed into the next. Around those sit the two materials. The medieval one has a glossy surface, a wide lime joint and tiles turned and shifted by hand. The Victorian one is matte, with a thin cement joint and tiles that sit almost exactly in their cells.
